{
  "topic": "Types of Fractions",
  "affirmation": "You made fractions feel real with models, warmth, and clear labels.",
  "identity": "You teach by showing and talking the math out loud: you put shapes on the board, asked students to count parts, and named the math words so the class could say them back. Your voice is conversational and grounding—mixing counting, a cake example, and quick checks to keep everyone involved.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Opening with a model",
      "quote": "چلیں، پہلے مجھے بتائیں کہ آپ کے سامنے میں ایک چیز دکھاؤں گی۔ آپ مجھے بتائیں، یہ کیا چیز ہے؟ یہ ایک ماڈل ہے، ٹھیک ہے؟ یہ کیا ہے؟",
      "why": "This mattered because you used a concrete shape to anchor the lesson and invited the class to notice parts before moving into fractions."
    },
    {
      "title": "Naming the words",
      "quote": "آپ مجھے بتائیں، جو اوپر جو نمبر ہوتا ہے، اس کو کیا کہتے ہیں؟ نمبریٹر.",
      "why": "This mattered because you gave students the exact vocabulary (numerator/denominator) and elicited the class to repeat the technical terms."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cake and fairness",
      "quote": "پارٹ اس لیے لاتے ہیں اور ایکول پارٹس لاتے ہیں تاکہ اس میں لائن نہ آئے۔",
      "why": "This mattered because you connected the math to a real-life situation and a social reason kids understand, making the idea of equal parts memorable."
    }
  ],
  "strength_name": "Concrete models & vocabulary",
  "strength_note": "You repeatedly drew and partitioned shapes, had students count shaded and total parts, and labeled numerator and denominator aloud so the math stayed visible.",
  "horizon_title": "Offer method choices",
  "horizon_note": "Give students chances to pick how they show a solution and to explain their own method in their own words.",
  "journey_note": "In this single focused session you used strong visuals and steady checks and reached a solid 79% peak; those moves keep appearing and will compound with one added step.",
  "score_framing": "A 79% snapshot reflects real strength and one clear next horizon, not a final judgment.",
  "domain_whys": {
    "high_leverage_practices": "This is developing because you guided students through partitioning and labeled steps (for example, you asked how many parts and named numerator/denominator) — it's not full marks because students were not given opportunities to choose how to solve or to present their own unprompted reasoning.",
    "student_engagement": "This is strong because students spoke up, counted parts and described shaded pieces (for example a student said '10 میں سے 9' and another said 'جو کلر کیا ہوا ہے، وہ اوپر ہے') — it's not full marks because much of the talk focused on procedural or placement questions (for example 'میم، ٹیسٹ لکھیں؟' and 'ادھر لکھنا ہے؟') rather than deeper concept questions.",
    "teacher_subject_knowledge": "This is developing because you corrected and contrasted cases and linked visuals to terms (for example you clarified '9 ہم نے اوپر لکھنا ہے، اور 10؟') — it's not full marks because conceptual prompts received quick answers with limited think time and misconceptions were not explicitly unpacked in extended discussion."
  }
}
